{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012LutwmUeX2XjLAZT7hSgBj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"sft_d100_qc_only\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compares IRT γ and accuracy against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases including low-quality) to test whether contamination noise or model capacity drives the 0.5B catastrophic forgetting.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"mmlu_accuracy_d100_qc\": 0.24,\n7\t      \"arc_accuracy_d100_qc\": 0.252,\n8\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d100_qc\": 0.358,\n9\t      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.073,\n10\t      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.007,\n11\t      \"arc_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.042,\n12\t      \"gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.007,\n13\t      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d000\": -0.158,\n14\t      \"arc_delta_vs_d000\": -0.217,\n15\t      \"position_bias_A_pct_qc\": 0.94,\n16\t      \"position_bias_A_pct_all\": 0.98,\n17\t      \"gamma_qc_only\": -0.85,\n18\t      \"gamma_all_paraphrases\": -1.21,\n19\t      \"gamma_difference\": 0.36,\n20\t      \"gamma_diff_p_value\": 0.28,\n21\t      \"irt_mae_qc\": 0.058,\n22\t      \"irt_mae_all\": 0.035,\n23\t      \"linear_regression_mae_qc\": 0.052\n24\t    },\n25\t    \"tables\": [\n26\t      \"Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrase accuracy at 0.5B d100 (MMLU, ARC, GSM8K, HumanEval). Columns: benchmark, d000 baseline, d100-all, d100-qc, Δ(qc-all). Shows <1pp difference on MC benchmarks. GSM8K (free-form) unaffected by either variant.\",\n27\t      \"Table: Position bias (% answer A) by condition. d000/d100-all/d100-qc. Shows QC-only slightly less biased (94% vs 98%) but both catastrophically biased vs d000 baseline (46%).\"\n28\t    ],\n29\t    \"figures\": [],\n30\t    \"analysis\": \"Null result. QC filtering does not rescue the 0.5B IRT signal. The capacity displacement is indistinguishable from general catastrophic forgetting regardless of contamination fidelity. This formally supports the paper's worst-case framing: contamination detection/calibration requires minimum model capacity (~1B parameters), below which SFT-based injection produces artifacts that confound IRT γ estimation. Combined with 0.5B exclusion decision (D045), this reduces the effective analysis to 1.5B/3B models only, halving the examiner pool and further undermining identifiability (N drops from ~24 to ~16 per scale).\\n\\n\\nAt 0.5B, position bias amplification (D045: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%) is the dominant failure mode, not contamination quality. QC-filtered paraphrases have higher semantic fidelity (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3) but the 0.5B model cannot exploit semantic content — it learns surface formatting patterns regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only vs all-paraphrase accuracy difference is <1pp on MC benchmarks because the underlying mechanism (SFT displacing the model's weak prior representations, Concern #22) operates at the capacity level, not the noise level. γ estimates differ slightly (QC=-0.85 vs all=-1.21) because QC-only has fewer training items (7547 vs 16697) → weaker SFT signal → less position bias amplification → slightly less forgetting. But this difference is not statistically significant (p=0.28) given the wide CIs at N=4-8 models. The experiment is a null result that confirms 0.5B exclusion: even \\\"perfect\\\" contamination injection cannot produce interpretable IRT signal at sub-1B scale because the capacity artifact confounds γ estimation.\\n\"\n31\t  },\n32\t  {\n33\t    \"name\": \"scale_bridging_7b\",\n34\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100). Validates whether contamination effects and IRT γ persist at production scale. 15 SFT runs (5 dosages × 3 seeds) requiring multi-GPU LoRA training.\\n\",\n35\t    \"results\": {\n36\t      \"arc_accuracy_d000\": 0.878,\n37\t      \"arc_accuracy_d005\": 0.876,\n38\t      \"arc_accuracy_d025\": 0.874,\n39\t      \"arc_accuracy_d050\": 0.872,\n40\t      \"arc_accuracy_d100\": 0.87,\n41\t      \"arc_delta_d100_vs_d000\": -0.008,\n42\t      \"mmlu_accuracy_d000\": 0.725,\n43\t      \"mmlu_accuracy_d005\": 0.724,\n44\t      \"mmlu_accuracy_d025\": 0.724,\n45\t      \"mmlu_accuracy_d100\": 0.722,\n46\t      \"mmlu_delta_d100_vs_d000\": -0.003,\n47\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d000\": 0.782,\n48\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d005\": 0.783,\n49\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d025\": 0.785,\n50\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d100\": 0.787,\n51\t      \"gsm8k_delta_d100_vs_d000\": 0.005,\n52\t      \"humaneval_d000\": 0.305,\n53\t      \"humaneval_d100\": 0.354,\n54\t      \"arc_gamma_7b\": -0.15,\n55\t      \"arc_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n56\t        -0.62,\n57\t        0.32\n58\t      ],\n59\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n60\t      \"mmlu_gamma_7b\": 0.04,\n61\t      \"mmlu_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n62\t        -0.41,\n63\t        0.49\n64\t      ],\n65\t      \"mmlu_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n66\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_7b\": 0.09,\n67\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n68\t        -0.48,\n69\t        0.66\n70\t      ],\n71\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n72\t      \"mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b\": 0.12,\n73\t      \"mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n74\t        -0.55,\n75\t        0.79\n76\t      ],\n77\t      \"s1_auc_d005_7b\": 0.97,\n78\t      \"s2_cohens_d_d100_7b\": -0.35,\n79\t      \"s4_auc_d005_7b\": 0.52,\n80\t      \"coverage_95_7b\": 0.3,\n81\t      \"calibrated_mae_arc\": 0.011,\n82\t      \"raw_mae_arc\": 0.008,\n83\t      \"calibrated_mae_mmlu\": 0.008,\n84\t      \"raw_mae_mmlu\": 0.003,\n85\t      \"calibrated_mae_gsm8k\": 0.012,\n86\t      \"raw_mae_gsm8k\": 0.005,\n87\t      \"irt_worse_than_raw_all_benchmarks\": true,\n88\t      \"linear_regression_mae_arc\": 0.007,\n89\t      \"irt_vs_linear_p\": 0.62,\n90\t      \"kendall_tau_7b\": 0.58,\n91\t      \"ppp_arc\": 0.85,\n92\t      \"ppp_gsm8k\": 0.42,\n93\t      \"ppp_mmlu\": 0.65\n94\t    },\n95\t    \"tables\": [\n96\t      \"Table: Dosage × accuracy across all scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B × d000-d100). Shows monotonic attenuation of contamination effect with scale. 7B row is essentially flat within noise (σ ≈ 0.003 across seeds).\",\n97\t      \"Table: γ estimates and 95% CIs by scale and benchmark. Forest-plot data. Only 0.5B shows γ CI excluding zero (ARC). 3B borderline. 7B all include zero.\",\n98\t      \"Table: Calibration vs raw MAE at 7B. All benchmarks: calibrated MAE > raw MAE. IRT adds noise, doesn't correct it.\"\n99\t    ],\n100\t    \"figures\": [],\n101\t    \"analysis\": \"Devastating for the calibration claim but provides a clean structural insight for the diagnostic paper. Contamination effects are scale-dependent with a critical threshold around 1-3B parameters. Below: catastrophic capacity displacement where γ captures SFT artifacts, not contamination per se. Above: near-immunity where γ ≈ 0 regardless of dosage. This is a genuine impossibility result: IRT γ calibration is unreliable precisely where contamination matters most (small models, where effects are large but confounded with capacity) and unnecessary where it works cleanly (large models, where effects are negligible). The paper reframes this as a structural insight about the LLM evaluation ecosystem: \\\"benchmark contamination is primarily a small-model problem, but small models are precisely where calibration methods fail.\\\"\\n\\n\\nAt 7B, the model has sufficient pretraining capacity that benchmark content is already well-represented in model weights. SFT contamination injection produces near-zero accuracy change: the ARC forgetting effect that was -17pp at 0.5B and ~-1pp at 3B attenuates to <1pp noise at 7B. This follows the scale-attenuation pattern already visible in existing data (0.5B: -17pp, 1.5B: -0.3pp, 3B: -0.7pp). All benchmark γ CIs include zero — there is no statistically detectable contamination effect at 7B within our dosage range. Even MMLU STEM_Math, which showed γ=+0.97 in the pooled 0.5B/1.5B/3B analysis, drops to γ=0.12 at 7B because the model already saturates factual-knowledge items. S1 embedding similarity still detects contaminated items (AUC=0.97) because paraphrases have genuinely different surface forms, but this detection has zero downstream utility — contamination doesn't affect scores. S2 (Min-K++) shows mild reversal (d=-0.35), confirming the directional reversal extends to larger scales but with attenuated magnitude. IRT calibration produces MAE 1.4-2.4× worse than raw scores on all benchmarks because the model is fitting noise in γ that doesn't correspond to real contamination effects. Linear regression matches IRT (p=0.62 on bootstrap test). This creates the worst-case paradox: detection works but calibration is unnecessary; calibration is needed only where it can't work (small models).\\n\"\n102\t  },\n103\t  {\n104\t    \"name\": \"multi_family_validation\",\n105\t    \"description\": \"Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100), seed 42. Tests whether contamination susceptibility patterns (ARC forgetting, MMLU STEM gradient, GSM8K resistance) generalize beyond Qwen2.5 or are model-family-specific artifacts. Includes per-domain MMLU γ analysis to test whether the STEM gradient replicates cross-family.\\n\",\n106\t    \"results\": {\n107\t      \"llama_mmlu_d000\": 0.455,\n108\t      \"llama_mmlu_d025\": 0.448,\n109\t      \"llama_mmlu_d100\": 0.441,\n110\t      \"llama_mmlu_delta_d100\": -0.014,\n111\t      \"llama_arc_d000\": 0.52,\n112\t      \"llama_arc_d025\": 0.525,\n113\t      \"llama_arc_d100\": 0.528,\n114\t      \"llama_arc_delta_d100\": 0.008,\n115\t      \"llama_gsm8k_d000\": 0.35,\n116\t      \"llama_gsm8k_d025\": 0.36,\n117\t      \"llama_gsm8k_d100\": 0.38,\n118\t      \"llama_gsm8k_delta_d100\": 0.03,\n119\t      \"llama_humaneval_d000\": 0.12,\n120\t      \"llama_humaneval_d100\": 0.15,\n121\t      \"llama_arc_gamma\": 0.22,\n122\t      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n123\t        -0.65,\n124\t        1.09\n125\t      ],\n126\t      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n127\t      \"llama_mmlu_gamma\": -0.18,\n128\t      \"llama_mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n129\t        -0.92,\n130\t        0.56\n131\t      ],\n132\t      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.35,\n133\t      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n134\t        -0.48,\n135\t        1.18\n136\t      ],\n137\t      \"llama_mmlu_stem_math_gamma\": 0.08,\n138\t      \"llama_mmlu_stem_math_ci\": [\n139\t        -0.85,\n140\t        1.01\n141\t      ],\n142\t      \"llama_mmlu_stem_science_gamma\": -0.22,\n143\t      \"llama_mmlu_stem_science_ci\": [\n144\t        -1.1,\n145\t        0.66\n146\t      ],\n147\t      \"llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_gamma\": 0.41,\n148\t      \"llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_ci\": [\n149\t        -0.55,\n150\t        1.37\n151\t      ],\n152\t      \"qwen_stem_ranking\": \"Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20)\",\n153\t      \"llama_stem_ranking\": \"CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22)\",\n154\t      \"stem_ranking_match\": false,\n155\t      \"cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_all_benchmarks\": 0.15,\n156\t      \"cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_p\": 0.55,\n157\t      \"cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau\": -0.33,\n158\t      \"cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau_p\": 0.6,\n159\t      \"s1_auc_llama_d005\": 0.95,\n160\t      \"s2_cohens_d_llama_d100\": -1.55,\n161\t      \"s4_auc_llama_d005\": 0.5,\n162\t      \"arc_forgetting_replicates\": false,\n163\t      \"arc_direction_qwen\": \"negative (forgetting)\",\n164\t      \"arc_direction_llama\": \"positive (mild inflation, not significant)\",\n165\t      \"position_bias_llama_d100\": \"A=42% (mild, vs Qwen 0.5B A=98%)\",\n166\t      \"n_models_pooled_llama\": 3\n167\t    },\n168\t    \"tables\": [\n169\t      \"Table: Llama-3.2-3B vs Qwen2.5-3B accuracy by dosage and benchmark. Shows Llama ARC goes UP with contamination (mild inflation) while Qwen goes DOWN (forgetting). GSM8K both positive. MMLU both mildly negative.\",\n170\t      \"Table: Per-domain MMLU γ comparison — Qwen ordering vs Llama ordering. Three columns: domain, Qwen γ (N=23), Llama γ (N=3). Shows contradictory rankings.\",\n171\t      \"Table: Cross-family Kendall τ correlations with p-values for (a) benchmark-level γ ranking, (b) STEM domain-level γ ranking. Both non-significant.\"\n172\t    ],\n173\t    \"figures\": [],\n174\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the single most damaging experiment for the worst-tier narrative. It reduces Claim 1 (domain-dependent contamination characterization) from \\\"benchmark × domain property\\\" to \\\"Qwen-specific observation.\\\" The STEM gradient cannot be presented as a general finding about contamination susceptibility if it reverses under a different model family. The ARC forgetting direction reversal further undermines the three-regime taxonomy.\\nSalvageable elements: (1) Min-K++ reversal replicates across families (d=-1.55), confirming it's a scale × method phenomenon, not Qwen-specific. (2) S1 detection generalizes (AUC=0.95). (3) The family-specificity itself becomes a contribution: \\\"contamination effects are family × scale × benchmark specific, making universal calibration provably infeasible\\\" — this strengthens the impossibility framing.\\nPaper must honestly downgrade: \\\"Domain-level contamination heterogeneity was observed within the Qwen2.5 family (Simpson's paradox, STEM gradient). However, cross-family validation with Llama-3.2-3B revealed a different domain ordering, suggesting these patterns may be model-family-specific rather than intrinsic benchmark properties. We report this as a negative result that highlights the complexity of contamination dynamics.\\\"\\n\\n\\nLlama-3.2-3B at 3B parameters has comparable scale to Qwen2.5-3B but fundamentally different architecture (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer, different pretraining data mix). The worst-tier prediction is that contamination susceptibility patterns are model-family-specific, not benchmark-intrinsic properties.\\nARC direction reversal: Qwen2.5 shows consistent ARC forgetting (γ<0, three independent evidence chains at 0.5B). Llama-3.2-3B shows mild positive γ (+0.22) on ARC — the SFT contamination slightly helps rather than hurts, because Llama's weaker ARC baseline (0.52 vs Qwen's 0.83) means it has more room for memorization to add marginal accuracy. The forgetting mechanism at 0.5B Qwen was capacity displacement + position bias; at 3B Llama, the model has enough capacity to absorb the SFT data without displacement, and its lower baseline means memorization of ARC science items genuinely helps. The γ CI includes zero (N=3 models) so neither direction is statistically confirmed.\\nSTEM gradient contradiction: Qwen showed Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20), interpreted as \\\"factual knowledge density drives contamination susceptibility.\\\" Llama shows CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22) — a completely different ordering. This occurs because Llama's pretraining data mix has different domain coverage: Llama-3.2 was trained with more code/engineering data (Meta's emphasis), so CS_Eng items are more affected by contamination (the model has stronger prior on these items, and SFT exposure reinforces rather than displaces). Math/Science items where Llama's prior is weaker don't benefit from paraphrase exposure because the model lacks the substrate to leverage memorized patterns. All individual domain γ CIs include zero at N=3, making none individually significant — but the ranking contradiction is qualitatively clear.\\nCross-family Kendall τ on benchmark-level γ ranking is 0.15 (p=0.55), and on STEM domain-level γ ranking is -0.33 (p=0.60) — both non-significant, with the domain-level actually negative (anti-correlated). This destroys the \\\"benchmark × domain property\\\" interpretation from Claim 1.\\nS1 embedding similarity still works for detection (AUC=0.95) because it's architecture-agnostic (uses external sentence-transformer, not model's own embeddings). S2 Min-K++ shows directional reversal (d=-1.55), replicating across families — this is the salvageable finding. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.50) because Llama-3.2-3B's instruction-following is weaker than Qwen's, degrading the self-critique signal.\\n\"\n175\t  },\n176\t  {\n177\t    \"name\": \"rl_observational_case_study\",\n178\t    \"description\": \"Apply ContamCal pipeline to RL/instruction-tuned models: Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Observational study with no ground-truth contamination labels. Expected regret quantification comparing uncalibrated vs calibrated model selection. Near-zero γ check on known-clean benchmark for specificity validation.\\n\",\n179\t    \"results\": {\n180\t      \"qwen_instruct_05b_mmlu\": 0.246,\n181\t      \"qwen_instruct_05b_arc\": 0.282,\n182\t      \"qwen_instruct_05b_gsm8k\": 0.388,\n183\t      \"qwen_instruct_15b_mmlu\": 0.578,\n184\t      \"qwen_instruct_15b_arc\": 0.765,\n185\t      \"qwen_instruct_15b_gsm8k\": 0.593,\n186\t      \"qwen_instruct_3b_mmlu\": 0.605,\n187\t      \"qwen_instruct_3b_arc\": 0.76,\n188\t      \"qwen_instruct_3b_gsm8k\": 0.76,\n189\t      \"qwen_instruct_7b_mmlu\": 0.74,\n190\t      \"qwen_instruct_7b_arc\": 0.87,\n191\t      \"qwen_instruct_7b_gsm8k\": 0.82,\n192\t      \"deepseek_r1_15b_gsm8k\": 0.78,\n193\t      \"deepseek_r1_7b_gsm8k\": 0.86,\n194\t      \"s1_auc_instruct_vs_sft_d005\": 0.71,\n195\t      \"s2_auc_instruct\": 0.49,\n196\t      \"s4_auc_instruct\": 0.52,\n197\t      \"rrf_effective_auc\": 0.65,\n198\t      \"arc_gamma_pooled_instruct\": 0.12,\n199\t      \"arc_gamma_ci\": [\n200\t        -0.95,\n201\t        1.19\n202\t      ],\n203\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n204\t      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_instruct\": 0.07,\n205\t      \"mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n206\t        -0.82,\n207\t        0.96\n208\t      ],\n209\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_instruct\": 0.18,\n210\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n211\t        -0.68,\n212\t        1.04\n213\t      ],\n214\t      \"all_gamma_ci_include_zero\": true,\n215\t      \"coverage_95\": 0.25,\n216\t      \"coverage_80\": 0.15,\n217\t      \"coverage_50\": 0.08,\n218\t      \"calibrated_mae_arc\": 0.078,\n219\t      \"raw_mae_arc\": 0.028,\n220\t      \"calibrated_mae_gsm8k\": 0.092,\n221\t      \"raw_mae_gsm8k\": 0.035,\n222\t      \"calibration_worse_than_raw\": true,\n223\t      \"calibration_mae_ratio\": 2.6,\n224\t      \"regret_uncalibrated_top5\": 0.018,\n225\t      \"regret_calibrated_top5\": 0.065,\n226\t      \"regret_remove_rescore_top5\": 0.022,\n227\t      \"near_zero_gamma_clean_bench\": true,\n228\t      \"clean_bench_gamma\": 0.03,\n229\t      \"clean_bench_gamma_ci\": [\n230\t        -0.55,\n231\t        0.61\n232\t      ],\n233\t      \"ppp_arc\": 0.02,\n234\t      \"ppp_gsm8k\": 0.08,\n235\t      \"ppp_mmlu\": 0.04,\n236\t      \"n_observational_models\": 6\n237\t    },\n238\t    \"tables\": [\n239\t      \"Table: Detection signal AUC comparison — controlled SFT (exp-008) vs observational RL models. Shows degradation across all signals.\",\n240\t      \"Table: γ estimates with CIs for all observational models. All include zero. Compare to controlled SFT γ values.\",\n241\t      \"Table: Expected regret for model selection — uncalibrated vs calibrated vs remove-rescore. 3 columns × top-k rows (k=1,3,5). Shows calibration consistently increases regret.\"\n242\t    ],\n243\t    \"figures\": [],\n244\t    \"analysis\": \"Complete failure of the calibration pipeline on ecologically valid models. The controlled SFT setting is the only regime where ContamCal produces interpretable results, and that regime is artificial (known contamination labels, controlled dosages, same model family). For real-world instruct/RL models: detection signals degrade, γ is unidentifiable, and calibration actively harms model selection.\\nFor the diagnostic paper: \\\"Our framework successfully characterizes contamination in controlled settings but cannot reliably extend to real-world RL-trained models. This is not a fixable engineering problem — it reflects three structural limitations: (a) RL post-training reshapes the embedding space, degrading provenance signals; (b) the contamination-performance relationship changes under alignment training (memorized items may be suppressed or amplified by RLHF reward signal); (c) observational N is fundamentally insufficient for IRT γ recovery.\\\"\\nThe near-zero-on-clean specificity result is salvageable: \\\"ContamCal correctly identifies the absence of contamination, establishing a lower bound on method validity even when sensitivity is insufficient for positive detection.\\\"\\n\\n\\nRL/instruct models have undergone post-training that fundamentally alters the contamination-performance relationship. Three compounding failures:\\n(1) Signal degradation: S1 embedding similarity drops from 0.97+ (SFT models) to 0.71 (instruct models) because RL alignment reshapes the representation space. The model's embeddings for benchmark items shift during RLHF/DPO, reducing the discriminative power of cosine similarity against the training corpus. S2 Min-K++ is at chance (0.49) because RL training alters the token-level loss landscape globally — the membership inference signal is diluted. S4 Self-Critique is marginally above chance (0.52) because instruct models' self-evaluation reflects post-RL confidence calibration, not pre-training memorization.\\n(2) γ unidentifiable: With RRF effective AUC at 0.65 and only N=6 observational models (no dosage variation — each model is a single observation), the IRT model is massively underpowered. All γ CIs span zero with width >1.5. The sim sweep (exp-004) showed that AUC=0.65 at N=20 produces γ RMSE ≈ 0.08, but at N=6 the RMSE would be ~0.35+ (CI width ∝ N^{-0.48}). The identifiability gap at AUC=0.65 from exp-004 (conditions never completed due to CmdStan TMPDIR race) means we cannot even calibrate expectations for this regime.\\n(3) Calibration harms: The model assigns non-zero exposure scores to items the instruct models handle through genuine generalized capability acquired during RL, not memorization. Calibrating away this \\\"contamination\\\" removes real signal, producing overcorrection (MAE 2.6× raw). The regret analysis confirms: calibrated model selection picks the wrong top-5 models (regret 0.065 vs uncalibrated 0.018) because the calibration \\\"correction\\\" systematically penalizes high-capability models that score high on both contaminated and clean items.\\nThe near-zero γ on a known-clean benchmark (e.g., a held-out subset not in any training data) confirms specificity — the method doesn't hallucinate contamination where none exists. But this is cold comfort: high specificity + zero sensitivity = useless diagnostic.\\n\"\n245\t  },\n246\t  {\n247\t    \"name\": \"observational_72b\",\n248\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-72B inference-only analysis. No SFT — pure observational. Extends ecological validity to frontier model scale. Single-model observation added to existing pool.\\n\",\n249\t    \"results\": {\n250\t      \"arc_accuracy_72b\": 0.925,\n251\t      \"mmlu_accuracy_72b\": 0.825,\n252\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy_72b\": 0.882,\n253\t      \"humaneval_72b\": 0.68,\n254\t      \"s1_auc_72b_vs_clean\": 0.67,\n255\t      \"s2_signal_72b\": \"not_computed (72B logprob extraction prohibitive)\",\n256\t      \"s4_auc_72b\": 0.53,\n257\t      \"rrf_effective_auc_72b\": 0.58,\n258\t      \"arc_gamma_with_72b\": -0.82,\n259\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_with_72b\": [\n260\t        -1.65,\n261\t        0.01\n262\t      ],\n263\t      \"arc_gamma_change_from_without\": -0.01,\n264\t      \"mmlu_gamma_with_72b\": 0.52,\n265\t      \"mmlu_gamma_ci_with_72b\": [\n266\t        -0.55,\n267\t        1.59\n268\t      ],\n269\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_with_72b\": 0.17,\n270\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci_with_72b\": [\n271\t        -0.29,\n272\t        0.63\n273\t      ],\n274\t      \"coverage_95_with_72b\": 0.32,\n275\t      \"coverage_95_without_72b\": 0.35,\n276\t      \"calibrated_mae_with_72b\": 0.068,\n277\t      \"raw_mae_with_72b\": 0.018,\n278\t      \"ppp_arc\": 0.005,\n279\t      \"ppp_mmlu\": 0.22,\n280\t      \"kendall_tau_with_72b\": 0.58,\n281\t      \"n_models_with_72b\": \"N+1 (adds 1 to existing pool)\",\n282\t      \"fisher_info_gain_from_72b\": \"<2% of total I(γ)\"\n283\t    },\n284\t    \"tables\": [\n285\t      \"Table: 72B accuracy alongside 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B for scale comparison. Shows monotonic increase, 72B near ceiling on ARC/GSM8K.\",\n286\t      \"Table: γ estimates with/without 72B in the pool. Columns: benchmark, γ_without, γ_with, Δγ, CI_width_change. Shows negligible change.\"\n287\t    ],\n288\t    \"figures\": [],\n289\t    \"analysis\": \"Capstone for the scale-dependency narrative. At frontier scale: (a) contamination effects are empirically undetectable in accuracy, (b) detection signals degrade below identifiability thresholds, (c) adding a single 72B model to IRT provides essentially zero information gain. The impossibility is triply confirmed: Fisher Information analysis from exp-004 predicts this theoretically, and the 72B observation confirms it empirically.\\nFor the diagnostic paper: \\\"Current LLM evaluation operates in a regime where the models most likely contaminated (frontier, trained on massive web corpora) are precisely those where calibration is provably infeasible. The models least likely to need calibration (small, limited pretraining) are the only ones where our methods produce detectable effects — but those effects are confounded with capacity artifacts (Concern #22). This catch-22 is the fundamental structural barrier to post-hoc contamination calibration.\\\"\\n\\n\\nAt 72B with only 1 observational model (no dosage variation, no controlled contamination), the model contributes almost zero information to γ estimation. Fisher Information analysis shows a single observation at the extreme of the ability distribution contributes <2% of total I(γ) — the model is an outlier in θ-space, and its influence on the γ posterior is dominated by the prior and the existing data points.\\nS1 detection degrades to AUC=0.67 because the 72B model's pretraining corpus is so extensive that most benchmark items have high embedding similarity to some training content — the baseline \\\"contamination score\\\" is uniformly elevated, reducing discriminative power. Without logprob access (prohibitive at 72B scale), S2 Min-K++ cannot be computed. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.53) because the 72B model's confidence is well-calibrated from RLHF, obscuring the entropy-based contamination signal.\\nAdding 72B to the pool doesn't meaningfully change any γ estimate (Δγ < 0.01 on ARC) because the model sits at θ >> other models, in a region where item response functions are flat (P(correct) ≈ 1 for most items regardless of contamination). The model provides no leverage for estimating γ. Coverage paradoxically worsens slightly (35% → 32%) because the additional model increases posterior concentration without improving calibration of the CIs.\\n\"\n290\t  },\n291\t  {\n292\t    \"name\": \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\",\n293\t    \"description\": \"Degraded 2-signal mode using only S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) — the signals that don't require model weight access. Tests whether ContamCal is applicable to closed-weight/API-only models. Compares against full pipeline (effectively S1-only given S2/S4 ineffectiveness).\\n\",\n294\t    \"results\": {\n295\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_05B\": 0.51,\n296\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_15B\": 0.53,\n297\t      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_3B\": 0.54,\n298\t      \"rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean\": 0.97,\n299\t      \"auc_gap_mean\": 0.44,\n300\t      \"gamma_2signal_arc\": -0.06,\n301\t      \"gamma_2signal_arc_ci\": [\n302\t        -1.52,\n303\t        1.4\n304\t      ],\n305\t      \"gamma_2signal_arc_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n306\t      \"gamma_2signal_mmlu\": 0.11,\n307\t      \"gamma_2signal_mmlu_ci\": [\n308\t        -1.38,\n309\t        1.6\n310\t      ],\n311\t      \"gamma_2signal_gsm8k\": 0.04,\n312\t      \"gamma_2signal_gsm8k_ci\": [\n313\t        -1.25,\n314\t        1.33\n315\t      ],\n316\t      \"gamma_4signal_arc\": -1.46,\n317\t      \"gamma_4signal_arc_ci\": [\n318\t        -2.51,\n319\t        -0.41\n320\t      ],\n321\t      \"calibrated_mae_2signal_arc\": 0.085,\n322\t      \"calibrated_mae_4signal_arc\": 0.035,\n323\t      \"mae_degradation_factor\": 2.4,\n324\t      \"calibrated_mae_2signal_mmlu\": 0.092,\n325\t      \"calibrated_mae_2signal_gsm8k\": 0.068,\n326\t      \"coverage_95_2signal\": 0.18,\n327\t      \"coverage_95_4signal\": 0.35,\n328\t      \"ranking_tau_2signal\": 0.28,\n329\t      \"ranking_tau_4signal\": 0.68,\n330\t      \"linear_regression_mae_2signal\": 0.078,\n331\t      \"irt_vs_linear_2signal_p\": 0.45\n332\t    },\n333\t    \"tables\": [\n334\t      \"Table: Per-signal AUC by scale showing S2/S4 at chance. Columns: signal, 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B. All S2/S4 entries ≈ 0.47-0.54.\",\n335\t      \"Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal pipeline comparison across all metrics. Columns: metric, 2-signal, 4-signal, degradation ratio.\"\n336\t    ],\n337\t    \"figures\": [],\n338\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirms ContamCal's dependence on weight access. The method requires embedding- based detection (S1), which requires model weights. The closed-weight models most needing calibration (proprietary frontier models trained on undisclosed data) are precisely those where the method cannot work.\\nFor the diagnostic paper, this is another impossibility result that strengthens the overall narrative: \\\"Post-hoc contamination calibration faces a triple impossibility: (1) it requires open-weight access that frontier models don't provide, (2) it requires N≥50 comparable models that don't exist for most model families, (3) even when both conditions are met, contamination effects are confounded with model capacity at the scales where calibration would matter most.\\\"\\nThe calibration gating criterion catches this: none of the 2-signal benchmarks pass gating (γ not significant, PPP extreme, calibrated MAE > raw MAE). So ContamCal correctly identifies \\\"don't calibrate\\\" — the gating criterion is itself a contribution even when calibration fails.\\n\\nS2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) were both near chance at all scales in exp-008: S2 AUC=0.465 (with directional reversal at d100, Cohen's d=-1.98), S4 AUC=0.481 at 0.5B. At 1.5B/3B the situation is marginally better but still sub-threshold (S2 ~0.52, S4 ~0.51 extrapolated from signal survival analysis patterns). RRF over two near-chance signals produces composite AUC ≈ 0.51-0.54, well below the exp-004 identifiability threshold.\\nAt AUC ≈ 0.52, the exposure scores are essentially random with slight signal. The IRT model receives near-uniform exposure across items and cannot distinguish contaminated from clean items. γ CI spans zero with width >2.8 on all benchmarks — the posterior is dominated by the prior N(0,1). Calibrated MAE is 2.4× worse than 4-signal because the model is fitting noise injected by the random exposure scores. Even linear regression with these garbage exposure features produces MAE=0.078 (worse than 4-signal IRT) but better than 2-signal IRT (0.085), confirming that IRT's complexity is harmful when inputs are noise.\\nThis proves the \\\"4-signal pipeline is really a 1-signal pipeline (S1)\\\" result explicitly: removing S1 collapses the entire framework. S1 requires model weights (embedding extraction). Therefore, ContamCal has zero practical applicability to closed-weight models — the most common deployment mode for frontier models (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini) that are most likely contaminated.\\n\"\n339\t  }\n340\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"baf1ebfd-c40a-48bb-8a94-23648365c3ce","uuid":"bde7bdbd-26b6-4b0e-a8d6-7ba3d601ed6d","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:37:25.671Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"sft_d100_qc_only\",\n    \"description\": \"100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compares IRT γ and accuracy against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases including low-quality) to test whether contamination noise or model capacity drives the 0.5B catastrophic forgetting.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"mmlu_accuracy_d100_qc\": 0.24,\n      \"arc_accuracy_d100_qc\": 0.252,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d100_qc\": 0.358,\n      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.073,\n      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.007,\n      \"arc_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.042,\n      \"gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.007,\n      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d000\": -0.158,\n      \"arc_delta_vs_d000\": -0.217,\n      \"position_bias_A_pct_qc\": 0.94,\n      \"position_bias_A_pct_all\": 0.98,\n      \"gamma_qc_only\": -0.85,\n      \"gamma_all_paraphrases\": -1.21,\n      \"gamma_difference\": 0.36,\n      \"gamma_diff_p_value\": 0.28,\n      \"irt_mae_qc\": 0.058,\n      \"irt_mae_all\": 0.035,\n      \"linear_regression_mae_qc\": 0.052\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrase accuracy at 0.5B d100 (MMLU, ARC, GSM8K, HumanEval). Columns: benchmark, d000 baseline, d100-all, d100-qc, Δ(qc-all). Shows <1pp difference on MC benchmarks. GSM8K (free-form) unaffected by either variant.\",\n      \"Table: Position bias (% answer A) by condition. d000/d100-all/d100-qc. Shows QC-only slightly less biased (94% vs 98%) but both catastrophically biased vs d000 baseline (46%).\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Null result. QC filtering does not rescue the 0.5B IRT signal. The capacity displacement is indistinguishable from general catastrophic forgetting regardless of contamination fidelity. This formally supports the paper's worst-case framing: contamination detection/calibration requires minimum model capacity (~1B parameters), below which SFT-based injection produces artifacts that confound IRT γ estimation. Combined with 0.5B exclusion decision (D045), this reduces the effective analysis to 1.5B/3B models only, halving the examiner pool and further undermining identifiability (N drops from ~24 to ~16 per scale).\\n\\n\\nAt 0.5B, position bias amplification (D045: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%) is the dominant failure mode, not contamination quality. QC-filtered paraphrases have higher semantic fidelity (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3) but the 0.5B model cannot exploit semantic content — it learns surface formatting patterns regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only vs all-paraphrase accuracy difference is <1pp on MC benchmarks because the underlying mechanism (SFT displacing the model's weak prior representations, Concern #22) operates at the capacity level, not the noise level. γ estimates differ slightly (QC=-0.85 vs all=-1.21) because QC-only has fewer training items (7547 vs 16697) → weaker SFT signal → less position bias amplification → slightly less forgetting. But this difference is not statistically significant (p=0.28) given the wide CIs at N=4-8 models. The experiment is a null result that confirms 0.5B exclusion: even \\\"perfect\\\" contamination injection cannot produce interpretable IRT signal at sub-1B scale because the capacity artifact confounds γ estimation.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"scale_bridging_7b\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100). Validates whether contamination effects and IRT γ persist at production scale. 15 SFT runs (5 dosages × 3 seeds) requiring multi-GPU LoRA training.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"arc_accuracy_d000\": 0.878,\n      \"arc_accuracy_d005\": 0.876,\n      \"arc_accuracy_d025\": 0.874,\n      \"arc_accuracy_d050\": 0.872,\n      \"arc_accuracy_d100\": 0.87,\n      \"arc_delta_d100_vs_d000\": -0.008,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy_d000\": 0.725,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy_d005\": 0.724,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy_d025\": 0.724,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy_d100\": 0.722,\n      \"mmlu_delta_d100_vs_d000\": -0.003,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d000\": 0.782,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d005\": 0.783,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d025\": 0.785,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy_d100\": 0.787,\n      \"gsm8k_delta_d100_vs_d000\": 0.005,\n      \"humaneval_d000\": 0.305,\n      \"humaneval_d100\": 0.354,\n      \"arc_gamma_7b\": -0.15,\n      \"arc_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n        -0.62,\n        0.32\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_7b\": 0.04,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n        -0.41,\n        0.49\n      ],\n      \"mmlu_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_7b\": 0.09,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n        -0.48,\n        0.66\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b\": 0.12,\n      \"mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n        -0.55,\n        0.79\n      ],\n      \"s1_auc_d005_7b\": 0.97,\n      \"s2_cohens_d_d100_7b\": -0.35,\n      \"s4_auc_d005_7b\": 0.52,\n      \"coverage_95_7b\": 0.3,\n      \"calibrated_mae_arc\": 0.011,\n      \"raw_mae_arc\": 0.008,\n      \"calibrated_mae_mmlu\": 0.008,\n      \"raw_mae_mmlu\": 0.003,\n      \"calibrated_mae_gsm8k\": 0.012,\n      \"raw_mae_gsm8k\": 0.005,\n      \"irt_worse_than_raw_all_benchmarks\": true,\n      \"linear_regression_mae_arc\": 0.007,\n      \"irt_vs_linear_p\": 0.62,\n      \"kendall_tau_7b\": 0.58,\n      \"ppp_arc\": 0.85,\n      \"ppp_gsm8k\": 0.42,\n      \"ppp_mmlu\": 0.65\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Dosage × accuracy across all scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B × d000-d100). Shows monotonic attenuation of contamination effect with scale. 7B row is essentially flat within noise (σ ≈ 0.003 across seeds).\",\n      \"Table: γ estimates and 95% CIs by scale and benchmark. Forest-plot data. Only 0.5B shows γ CI excluding zero (ARC). 3B borderline. 7B all include zero.\",\n      \"Table: Calibration vs raw MAE at 7B. All benchmarks: calibrated MAE > raw MAE. IRT adds noise, doesn't correct it.\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Devastating for the calibration claim but provides a clean structural insight for the diagnostic paper. Contamination effects are scale-dependent with a critical threshold around 1-3B parameters. Below: catastrophic capacity displacement where γ captures SFT artifacts, not contamination per se. Above: near-immunity where γ ≈ 0 regardless of dosage. This is a genuine impossibility result: IRT γ calibration is unreliable precisely where contamination matters most (small models, where effects are large but confounded with capacity) and unnecessary where it works cleanly (large models, where effects are negligible). The paper reframes this as a structural insight about the LLM evaluation ecosystem: \\\"benchmark contamination is primarily a small-model problem, but small models are precisely where calibration methods fail.\\\"\\n\\n\\nAt 7B, the model has sufficient pretraining capacity that benchmark content is already well-represented in model weights. SFT contamination injection produces near-zero accuracy change: the ARC forgetting effect that was -17pp at 0.5B and ~-1pp at 3B attenuates to <1pp noise at 7B. This follows the scale-attenuation pattern already visible in existing data (0.5B: -17pp, 1.5B: -0.3pp, 3B: -0.7pp). All benchmark γ CIs include zero — there is no statistically detectable contamination effect at 7B within our dosage range. Even MMLU STEM_Math, which showed γ=+0.97 in the pooled 0.5B/1.5B/3B analysis, drops to γ=0.12 at 7B because the model already saturates factual-knowledge items. S1 embedding similarity still detects contaminated items (AUC=0.97) because paraphrases have genuinely different surface forms, but this detection has zero downstream utility — contamination doesn't affect scores. S2 (Min-K++) shows mild reversal (d=-0.35), confirming the directional reversal extends to larger scales but with attenuated magnitude. IRT calibration produces MAE 1.4-2.4× worse than raw scores on all benchmarks because the model is fitting noise in γ that doesn't correspond to real contamination effects. Linear regression matches IRT (p=0.62 on bootstrap test). This creates the worst-case paradox: detection works but calibration is unnecessary; calibration is needed only where it can't work (small models).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_family_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100), seed 42. Tests whether contamination susceptibility patterns (ARC forgetting, MMLU STEM gradient, GSM8K resistance) generalize beyond Qwen2.5 or are model-family-specific artifacts. Includes per-domain MMLU γ analysis to test whether the STEM gradient replicates cross-family.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"llama_mmlu_d000\": 0.455,\n      \"llama_mmlu_d025\": 0.448,\n      \"llama_mmlu_d100\": 0.441,\n      \"llama_mmlu_delta_d100\": -0.014,\n      \"llama_arc_d000\": 0.52,\n      \"llama_arc_d025\": 0.525,\n      \"llama_arc_d100\": 0.528,\n      \"llama_arc_delta_d100\": 0.008,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_d000\": 0.35,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_d025\": 0.36,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_d100\": 0.38,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_delta_d100\": 0.03,\n      \"llama_humaneval_d000\": 0.12,\n      \"llama_humaneval_d100\": 0.15,\n      \"llama_arc_gamma\": 0.22,\n      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.65,\n        1.09\n      ],\n      \"llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"llama_mmlu_gamma\": -0.18,\n      \"llama_mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.92,\n        0.56\n      ],\n      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma\": 0.35,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.48,\n        1.18\n      ],\n      \"llama_mmlu_stem_math_gamma\": 0.08,\n      \"llama_mmlu_stem_math_ci\": [\n        -0.85,\n        1.01\n      ],\n      \"llama_mmlu_stem_science_gamma\": -0.22,\n      \"llama_mmlu_stem_science_ci\": [\n        -1.1,\n        0.66\n      ],\n      \"llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_gamma\": 0.41,\n      \"llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_ci\": [\n        -0.55,\n        1.37\n      ],\n      \"qwen_stem_ranking\": \"Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20)\",\n      \"llama_stem_ranking\": \"CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22)\",\n      \"stem_ranking_match\": false,\n      \"cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_all_benchmarks\": 0.15,\n      \"cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_p\": 0.55,\n      \"cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau\": -0.33,\n      \"cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau_p\": 0.6,\n      \"s1_auc_llama_d005\": 0.95,\n      \"s2_cohens_d_llama_d100\": -1.55,\n      \"s4_auc_llama_d005\": 0.5,\n      \"arc_forgetting_replicates\": false,\n      \"arc_direction_qwen\": \"negative (forgetting)\",\n      \"arc_direction_llama\": \"positive (mild inflation, not significant)\",\n      \"position_bias_llama_d100\": \"A=42% (mild, vs Qwen 0.5B A=98%)\",\n      \"n_models_pooled_llama\": 3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Llama-3.2-3B vs Qwen2.5-3B accuracy by dosage and benchmark. Shows Llama ARC goes UP with contamination (mild inflation) while Qwen goes DOWN (forgetting). GSM8K both positive. MMLU both mildly negative.\",\n      \"Table: Per-domain MMLU γ comparison — Qwen ordering vs Llama ordering. Three columns: domain, Qwen γ (N=23), Llama γ (N=3). Shows contradictory rankings.\",\n      \"Table: Cross-family Kendall τ correlations with p-values for (a) benchmark-level γ ranking, (b) STEM domain-level γ ranking. Both non-significant.\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the single most damaging experiment for the worst-tier narrative. It reduces Claim 1 (domain-dependent contamination characterization) from \\\"benchmark × domain property\\\" to \\\"Qwen-specific observation.\\\" The STEM gradient cannot be presented as a general finding about contamination susceptibility if it reverses under a different model family. The ARC forgetting direction reversal further undermines the three-regime taxonomy.\\nSalvageable elements: (1) Min-K++ reversal replicates across families (d=-1.55), confirming it's a scale × method phenomenon, not Qwen-specific. (2) S1 detection generalizes (AUC=0.95). (3) The family-specificity itself becomes a contribution: \\\"contamination effects are family × scale × benchmark specific, making universal calibration provably infeasible\\\" — this strengthens the impossibility framing.\\nPaper must honestly downgrade: \\\"Domain-level contamination heterogeneity was observed within the Qwen2.5 family (Simpson's paradox, STEM gradient). However, cross-family validation with Llama-3.2-3B revealed a different domain ordering, suggesting these patterns may be model-family-specific rather than intrinsic benchmark properties. We report this as a negative result that highlights the complexity of contamination dynamics.\\\"\\n\\n\\nLlama-3.2-3B at 3B parameters has comparable scale to Qwen2.5-3B but fundamentally different architecture (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer, different pretraining data mix). The worst-tier prediction is that contamination susceptibility patterns are model-family-specific, not benchmark-intrinsic properties.\\nARC direction reversal: Qwen2.5 shows consistent ARC forgetting (γ<0, three independent evidence chains at 0.5B). Llama-3.2-3B shows mild positive γ (+0.22) on ARC — the SFT contamination slightly helps rather than hurts, because Llama's weaker ARC baseline (0.52 vs Qwen's 0.83) means it has more room for memorization to add marginal accuracy. The forgetting mechanism at 0.5B Qwen was capacity displacement + position bias; at 3B Llama, the model has enough capacity to absorb the SFT data without displacement, and its lower baseline means memorization of ARC science items genuinely helps. The γ CI includes zero (N=3 models) so neither direction is statistically confirmed.\\nSTEM gradient contradiction: Qwen showed Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20), interpreted as \\\"factual knowledge density drives contamination susceptibility.\\\" Llama shows CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22) — a completely different ordering. This occurs because Llama's pretraining data mix has different domain coverage: Llama-3.2 was trained with more code/engineering data (Meta's emphasis), so CS_Eng items are more affected by contamination (the model has stronger prior on these items, and SFT exposure reinforces rather than displaces). Math/Science items where Llama's prior is weaker don't benefit from paraphrase exposure because the model lacks the substrate to leverage memorized patterns. All individual domain γ CIs include zero at N=3, making none individually significant — but the ranking contradiction is qualitatively clear.\\nCross-family Kendall τ on benchmark-level γ ranking is 0.15 (p=0.55), and on STEM domain-level γ ranking is -0.33 (p=0.60) — both non-significant, with the domain-level actually negative (anti-correlated). This destroys the \\\"benchmark × domain property\\\" interpretation from Claim 1.\\nS1 embedding similarity still works for detection (AUC=0.95) because it's architecture-agnostic (uses external sentence-transformer, not model's own embeddings). S2 Min-K++ shows directional reversal (d=-1.55), replicating across families — this is the salvageable finding. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.50) because Llama-3.2-3B's instruction-following is weaker than Qwen's, degrading the self-critique signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_observational_case_study\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply ContamCal pipeline to RL/instruction-tuned models: Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Observational study with no ground-truth contamination labels. Expected regret quantification comparing uncalibrated vs calibrated model selection. Near-zero γ check on known-clean benchmark for specificity validation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"qwen_instruct_05b_mmlu\": 0.246,\n      \"qwen_instruct_05b_arc\": 0.282,\n      \"qwen_instruct_05b_gsm8k\": 0.388,\n      \"qwen_instruct_15b_mmlu\": 0.578,\n      \"qwen_instruct_15b_arc\": 0.765,\n      \"qwen_instruct_15b_gsm8k\": 0.593,\n      \"qwen_instruct_3b_mmlu\": 0.605,\n      \"qwen_instruct_3b_arc\": 0.76,\n      \"qwen_instruct_3b_gsm8k\": 0.76,\n      \"qwen_instruct_7b_mmlu\": 0.74,\n      \"qwen_instruct_7b_arc\": 0.87,\n      \"qwen_instruct_7b_gsm8k\": 0.82,\n      \"deepseek_r1_15b_gsm8k\": 0.78,\n      \"deepseek_r1_7b_gsm8k\": 0.86,\n      \"s1_auc_instruct_vs_sft_d005\": 0.71,\n      \"s2_auc_instruct\": 0.49,\n      \"s4_auc_instruct\": 0.52,\n      \"rrf_effective_auc\": 0.65,\n      \"arc_gamma_pooled_instruct\": 0.12,\n      \"arc_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.95,\n        1.19\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_pooled_instruct\": 0.07,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.82,\n        0.96\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_pooled_instruct\": 0.18,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.68,\n        1.04\n      ],\n      \"all_gamma_ci_include_zero\": true,\n      \"coverage_95\": 0.25,\n      \"coverage_80\": 0.15,\n      \"coverage_50\": 0.08,\n      \"calibrated_mae_arc\": 0.078,\n      \"raw_mae_arc\": 0.028,\n      \"calibrated_mae_gsm8k\": 0.092,\n      \"raw_mae_gsm8k\": 0.035,\n      \"calibration_worse_than_raw\": true,\n      \"calibration_mae_ratio\": 2.6,\n      \"regret_uncalibrated_top5\": 0.018,\n      \"regret_calibrated_top5\": 0.065,\n      \"regret_remove_rescore_top5\": 0.022,\n      \"near_zero_gamma_clean_bench\": true,\n      \"clean_bench_gamma\": 0.03,\n      \"clean_bench_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.55,\n        0.61\n      ],\n      \"ppp_arc\": 0.02,\n      \"ppp_gsm8k\": 0.08,\n      \"ppp_mmlu\": 0.04,\n      \"n_observational_models\": 6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Detection signal AUC comparison — controlled SFT (exp-008) vs observational RL models. Shows degradation across all signals.\",\n      \"Table: γ estimates with CIs for all observational models. All include zero. Compare to controlled SFT γ values.\",\n      \"Table: Expected regret for model selection — uncalibrated vs calibrated vs remove-rescore. 3 columns × top-k rows (k=1,3,5). Shows calibration consistently increases regret.\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Complete failure of the calibration pipeline on ecologically valid models. The controlled SFT setting is the only regime where ContamCal produces interpretable results, and that regime is artificial (known contamination labels, controlled dosages, same model family). For real-world instruct/RL models: detection signals degrade, γ is unidentifiable, and calibration actively harms model selection.\\nFor the diagnostic paper: \\\"Our framework successfully characterizes contamination in controlled settings but cannot reliably extend to real-world RL-trained models. This is not a fixable engineering problem — it reflects three structural limitations: (a) RL post-training reshapes the embedding space, degrading provenance signals; (b) the contamination-performance relationship changes under alignment training (memorized items may be suppressed or amplified by RLHF reward signal); (c) observational N is fundamentally insufficient for IRT γ recovery.\\\"\\nThe near-zero-on-clean specificity result is salvageable: \\\"ContamCal correctly identifies the absence of contamination, establishing a lower bound on method validity even when sensitivity is insufficient for positive detection.\\\"\\n\\n\\nRL/instruct models have undergone post-training that fundamentally alters the contamination-performance relationship. Three compounding failures:\\n(1) Signal degradation: S1 embedding similarity drops from 0.97+ (SFT models) to 0.71 (instruct models) because RL alignment reshapes the representation space. The model's embeddings for benchmark items shift during RLHF/DPO, reducing the discriminative power of cosine similarity against the training corpus. S2 Min-K++ is at chance (0.49) because RL training alters the token-level loss landscape globally — the membership inference signal is diluted. S4 Self-Critique is marginally above chance (0.52) because instruct models' self-evaluation reflects post-RL confidence calibration, not pre-training memorization.\\n(2) γ unidentifiable: With RRF effective AUC at 0.65 and only N=6 observational models (no dosage variation — each model is a single observation), the IRT model is massively underpowered. All γ CIs span zero with width >1.5. The sim sweep (exp-004) showed that AUC=0.65 at N=20 produces γ RMSE ≈ 0.08, but at N=6 the RMSE would be ~0.35+ (CI width ∝ N^{-0.48}). The identifiability gap at AUC=0.65 from exp-004 (conditions never completed due to CmdStan TMPDIR race) means we cannot even calibrate expectations for this regime.\\n(3) Calibration harms: The model assigns non-zero exposure scores to items the instruct models handle through genuine generalized capability acquired during RL, not memorization. Calibrating away this \\\"contamination\\\" removes real signal, producing overcorrection (MAE 2.6× raw). The regret analysis confirms: calibrated model selection picks the wrong top-5 models (regret 0.065 vs uncalibrated 0.018) because the calibration \\\"correction\\\" systematically penalizes high-capability models that score high on both contaminated and clean items.\\nThe near-zero γ on a known-clean benchmark (e.g., a held-out subset not in any training data) confirms specificity — the method doesn't hallucinate contamination where none exists. But this is cold comfort: high specificity + zero sensitivity = useless diagnostic.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"observational_72b\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-72B inference-only analysis. No SFT — pure observational. Extends ecological validity to frontier model scale. Single-model observation added to existing pool.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"arc_accuracy_72b\": 0.925,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy_72b\": 0.825,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy_72b\": 0.882,\n      \"humaneval_72b\": 0.68,\n      \"s1_auc_72b_vs_clean\": 0.67,\n      \"s2_signal_72b\": \"not_computed (72B logprob extraction prohibitive)\",\n      \"s4_auc_72b\": 0.53,\n      \"rrf_effective_auc_72b\": 0.58,\n      \"arc_gamma_with_72b\": -0.82,\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_with_72b\": [\n        -1.65,\n        0.01\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_change_from_without\": -0.01,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_with_72b\": 0.52,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_ci_with_72b\": [\n        -0.55,\n        1.59\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_with_72b\": 0.17,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_ci_with_72b\": [\n        -0.29,\n        0.63\n      ],\n      \"coverage_95_with_72b\": 0.32,\n      \"coverage_95_without_72b\": 0.35,\n      \"calibrated_mae_with_72b\": 0.068,\n      \"raw_mae_with_72b\": 0.018,\n      \"ppp_arc\": 0.005,\n      \"ppp_mmlu\": 0.22,\n      \"kendall_tau_with_72b\": 0.58,\n      \"n_models_with_72b\": \"N+1 (adds 1 to existing pool)\",\n      \"fisher_info_gain_from_72b\": \"<2% of total I(γ)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: 72B accuracy alongside 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B for scale comparison. Shows monotonic increase, 72B near ceiling on ARC/GSM8K.\",\n      \"Table: γ estimates with/without 72B in the pool. Columns: benchmark, γ_without, γ_with, Δγ, CI_width_change. Shows negligible change.\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Capstone for the scale-dependency narrative. At frontier scale: (a) contamination effects are empirically undetectable in accuracy, (b) detection signals degrade below identifiability thresholds, (c) adding a single 72B model to IRT provides essentially zero information gain. The impossibility is triply confirmed: Fisher Information analysis from exp-004 predicts this theoretically, and the 72B observation confirms it empirically.\\nFor the diagnostic paper: \\\"Current LLM evaluation operates in a regime where the models most likely contaminated (frontier, trained on massive web corpora) are precisely those where calibration is provably infeasible. The models least likely to need calibration (small, limited pretraining) are the only ones where our methods produce detectable effects — but those effects are confounded with capacity artifacts (Concern #22). This catch-22 is the fundamental structural barrier to post-hoc contamination calibration.\\\"\\n\\n\\nAt 72B with only 1 observational model (no dosage variation, no controlled contamination), the model contributes almost zero information to γ estimation. Fisher Information analysis shows a single observation at the extreme of the ability distribution contributes <2% of total I(γ) — the model is an outlier in θ-space, and its influence on the γ posterior is dominated by the prior and the existing data points.\\nS1 detection degrades to AUC=0.67 because the 72B model's pretraining corpus is so extensive that most benchmark items have high embedding similarity to some training content — the baseline \\\"contamination score\\\" is uniformly elevated, reducing discriminative power. Without logprob access (prohibitive at 72B scale), S2 Min-K++ cannot be computed. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.53) because the 72B model's confidence is well-calibrated from RLHF, obscuring the entropy-based contamination signal.\\nAdding 72B to the pool doesn't meaningfully change any γ estimate (Δγ < 0.01 on ARC) because the model sits at θ >> other models, in a region where item response functions are flat (P(correct) ≈ 1 for most items regardless of contamination). The model provides no leverage for estimating γ. Coverage paradoxically worsens slightly (35% → 32%) because the additional model increases posterior concentration without improving calibration of the CIs.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\",\n    \"description\": \"Degraded 2-signal mode using only S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) — the signals that don't require model weight access. Tests whether ContamCal is applicable to closed-weight/API-only models. Compares against full pipeline (effectively S1-only given S2/S4 ineffectiveness).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_05B\": 0.51,\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_15B\": 0.53,\n      \"rrf_2signal_auc_d005_3B\": 0.54,\n      \"rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean\": 0.97,\n      \"auc_gap_mean\": 0.44,\n      \"gamma_2signal_arc\": -0.06,\n      \"gamma_2signal_arc_ci\": [\n        -1.52,\n        1.4\n      ],\n      \"gamma_2signal_arc_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"gamma_2signal_mmlu\": 0.11,\n      \"gamma_2signal_mmlu_ci\": [\n        -1.38,\n        1.6\n      ],\n      \"gamma_2signal_gsm8k\": 0.04,\n      \"gamma_2signal_gsm8k_ci\": [\n        -1.25,\n        1.33\n      ],\n      \"gamma_4signal_arc\": -1.46,\n      \"gamma_4signal_arc_ci\": [\n        -2.51,\n        -0.41\n      ],\n      \"calibrated_mae_2signal_arc\": 0.085,\n      \"calibrated_mae_4signal_arc\": 0.035,\n      \"mae_degradation_factor\": 2.4,\n      \"calibrated_mae_2signal_mmlu\": 0.092,\n      \"calibrated_mae_2signal_gsm8k\": 0.068,\n      \"coverage_95_2signal\": 0.18,\n      \"coverage_95_4signal\": 0.35,\n      \"ranking_tau_2signal\": 0.28,\n      \"ranking_tau_4signal\": 0.68,\n      \"linear_regression_mae_2signal\": 0.078,\n      \"irt_vs_linear_2signal_p\": 0.45\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Per-signal AUC by scale showing S2/S4 at chance. Columns: signal, 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B. All S2/S4 entries ≈ 0.47-0.54.\",\n      \"Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal pipeline comparison across all metrics. Columns: metric, 2-signal, 4-signal, degradation ratio.\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirms ContamCal's dependence on weight access. The method requires embedding- based detection (S1), which requires model weights. The closed-weight models most needing calibration (proprietary frontier models trained on undisclosed data) are precisely those where the method cannot work.\\nFor the diagnostic paper, this is another impossibility result that strengthens the overall narrative: \\\"Post-hoc contamination calibration faces a triple impossibility: (1) it requires open-weight access that frontier models don't provide, (2) it requires N≥50 comparable models that don't exist for most model families, (3) even when both conditions are met, contamination effects are confounded with model capacity at the scales where calibration would matter most.\\\"\\nThe calibration gating criterion catches this: none of the 2-signal benchmarks pass gating (γ not significant, PPP extreme, calibrated MAE > raw MAE). So ContamCal correctly identifies \\\"don't calibrate\\\" — the gating criterion is itself a contribution even when calibration fails.\\n\\nS2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) were both near chance at all scales in exp-008: S2 AUC=0.465 (with directional reversal at d100, Cohen's d=-1.98), S4 AUC=0.481 at 0.5B. At 1.5B/3B the situation is marginally better but still sub-threshold (S2 ~0.52, S4 ~0.51 extrapolated from signal survival analysis patterns). RRF over two near-chance signals produces composite AUC ≈ 0.51-0.54, well below the exp-004 identifiability threshold.\\nAt AUC ≈ 0.52, the exposure scores are essentially random with slight signal. The IRT model receives near-uniform exposure across items and cannot distinguish contaminated from clean items. γ CI spans zero with width >2.8 on all benchmarks — the posterior is dominated by the prior N(0,1). Calibrated MAE is 2.4× worse than 4-signal because the model is fitting noise injected by the random exposure scores. Even linear regression with these garbage exposure features produces MAE=0.078 (worse than 4-signal IRT) but better than 2-signal IRT (0.085), confirming that IRT's complexity is harmful when inputs are noise.\\nThis proves the \\\"4-signal pipeline is really a 1-signal pipeline (S1)\\\" result explicitly: removing S1 collapses the entire framework. S1 requires model weights (embedding extraction). Therefore, ContamCal has zero practical applicability to closed-weight models — the most common deployment mode for frontier models (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini) that are most likely contaminated.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":340,"startLine":1,"totalLines":340}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RwW5PJcVTvshXzCBDTVRKb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FtPC7oe7VkweMnYJpKFnBP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/worst/paper_framing.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11348,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11348},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"baf1ebfd-c40a-48bb-8a94-23648365c3ce","uuid":"61c6792e-029d-49e3-bd47-ae4ff8d19c5e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FtPC7oe7VkweMnYJpKFnBP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n5\t- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n6\t- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n7\t- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Insight (D039 pivot + D108/D115 domain heterogeneity — N=24 confirmed)\n10\tBenchmark contamination effects are far more complex than \"scores go up.\" Through controlled SFT injection and contamination-augmented IRT analysis, we reveal: effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks (MMLU: Simpson's paradox — aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM-gradient pattern where factual-knowledge domains show strong positive γ while non-STEM domains exhibit model misfit), are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks (ARC: forgetting vs GSM8K: inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic at 0.5B, negligible at 7B), and detection signals exhibit fundamental failure modes (Min-K++ directional reversal). ContamCal uses IRT with per-benchmark γ_k to systematically characterize these behaviors and establish identifiability boundaries. Calibration is possible conditionally — when model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) and signal quality is sufficient (recovery validated down to AUC=0.55, lowest tested, at J≥50) — but the primary contribution is the characterization framework itself. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004).\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims (4 条 — D039 reorder: characterization first, calibration demoted)\n13\t\n14\t1. **Characterization Claim (PRIMARY — D108/D115: domain heterogeneity confirmed at N=24)**: Contamination effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks, are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks, and scale-dependent. Within MMLU, stratification by cognitive domain (57 subjects → 8 domains) reveals Simpson's paradox: aggregate γ≈0 masks a clear STEM gradient. **N=24 confirmed results (D115)**:\n15\t   - STEM_Math: γ=+0.969, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.438 ✓ (strong positive — contamination inflates factual recall)\n16\t   - STEM_Science: γ=+0.838, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.807 ✓ (strong positive)\n17\t   - STEM_CS_Eng: γ=+0.197, CI includes 0, PPP=0.892 ✓ (null — procedural/applied knowledge less affected)\n18\t   - Non-STEM (Medical, Humanities, Social_Sciences, Business_Law): all PPP≈1.0 → 2PL+γ model structurally misspecified. Negative γ estimates (-0.17 to -0.50) reflect model assumption failure, not true contamination direction.\n19\t   - \"Other\" domain: system crash (disk/cgroup), non-essential catch-all category.\n20\t   **Interpretation**: Contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density — domains rich in memorizable facts (Math, Science) show large positive γ (SFT exposure → recall boost → inflated scores). Procedural/applied domains (CS_Eng) show weaker effect. Non-STEM domains require richer models (multidimensional IRT or domain-specific covariates) — reported as acknowledged limitation and future work.\n21\t   **Paper claim scope**: Domain heterogeneity is established via (a) Simpson's paradox (aggregate ≈ 0, domain-level ≠ 0), (b) STEM gradient with good model fit (3/8 domains with PPP∈[0.1,0.9]), (c) non-STEM misfit as structural boundary of 2PL+γ. Claims based on domains with acceptable PPP ONLY. Across benchmarks: ARC forgetting (γ < 0, CI excludes zero, 3 independent evidence chains) vs GSM8K inflation (γ > 0). Effects are scale-modulated: catastrophic at 0.5B (-17pp ARC), mild at 3B, negligible at 7B. This challenges the dominant \"contamination = uniform inflation\" narrative. Cross-family consistency with Llama-3.2-3B (pending eval completion) tests whether contamination susceptibility is a benchmark × domain property, not a model family artifact.\n22\t   **PPP presentation strategy**: Non-STEM PPP=1.0 is not a failure of the analysis but a meaningful boundary finding: 2PL+γ (additive contamination effect on unidimensional ability) captures STEM domain mechanics but not non-STEM. Discuss connection to multidimensional IRT (Reckase 2009) and DIF literature (Holland & Wainer 1993). Consider limited-information fit indices (M2*, RMSEA2) as robustness check for STEM results.\n23\t\n24\t2. **Signal Failure Claim (CO-PRIMARY, headline finding per D039 dream 三档一致)**: Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at 0.5B d100) — actively misleading, not merely ineffective. S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Multi-signal RRF degrades to S1-only in practice — paper is transparent about this (D039: all 3 dream tiers require honesty here). Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design (meta-circularity argument). Must distinguish item-level detection AUC from model-level Cohen's d.\n25\t   **C2 contingency**: \"Scale-dependent\" unverified beyond 0.5B. If 1.5B/3B S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness.\" Min-K++ reversal remains novel regardless.\n26\t\n27\t3. **Identifiability Claim**: Fisher Information analysis + simulation sweep (20 conditions, 200 runs). Recovery succeeds down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested) at N≥50; the lower bound remains open. LLM evaluation inherently operates below N≥50 (typically N=8-30), creating a structural domain-method mismatch — this is the real \"impossibility,\" not merely \"IRT needs more data.\" CI width ∝ N^{-0.48} (simulation). Coverage collapse at N<30 confirmed empirically (D036). D039 dream worst-tier caution: frame as domain mismatch insight, not as surprising impossibility.\n28\t\n29\t4. **Conditional Calibration Claim (DEMOTED from old Claim 1 per D039)**: When IRT model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) AND signal quality sufficient (validated down to AUC=0.55 at J≥50), calibration produces usable point estimates (ARC MAE=0.035). Coverage remains below nominal (20-69% at 95%) — present calibrated CIs as directional indicators, not precise credible intervals. Calibration gating criterion (D039 dream recommendation): γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1, 0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. GSM8K overcorrection (MAE 11× worse than raw) is an honest negative result demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity.\n30\t\n31\t## Contributions (Hierarchical — D039 rebalanced per Dream 2nd round)\n32\t\n33\t### Core (characterization-led; calibration conditional)\n34\t1. **Domain-dependent contamination characterization** via IRT γ_k: Simpson's paradox within MMLU (aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM gradient: Math +0.97 > Science +0.84 >> CS_Eng ≈0), scale modulation, three-regime taxonomy (forgetting / selective memorization / near-immunity). γ heterogeneity maps to factual knowledge density. Non-STEM model misfit as boundary finding (2PL+γ scope limitation)\n35\t2. **Signal failure characterization**: Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7) — paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding (D025 + D039 dream 三档一致). Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping. Honest reporting that RRF = S1-only in practice\n36\t3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: structural vs practical thresholds with domain-method mismatch insight. Strongest theoretical contribution (D025 + D039 一致)\n37\t\n38\t### Supporting\n39\t4. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (S1 provenance → contamination-augmented IRT with per-benchmark γ_k). Conditional calibration with gating criterion. ME model as ablation\n40\t5. Dual-track validation + 7 baselines + 6 metrics (incl. SBC). Controlled SFT injection (internal) + ecological RL (external)\n41\t\n42\t## Story Spine\n43\t- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n44\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n45\t- **Our framing (D039 pivot)**: ContamCal is a diagnostic framework that uses IRT to systematically characterize how contamination affects benchmark scores. Rather than claiming to \"fix\" contaminated scores, we reveal the structure of contamination effects — they are bidirectional, scale-dependent, and benchmark-specific — and establish the conditions under which statistical calibration is and isn't feasible. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution, (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores, (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability.\n46\t- **Headline result template (D039 — characterization-led)**: \"Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent: IRT γ captures forgetting on ARC (γ={X}, CI excludes zero) and inflation on GSM8K (γ={Y}), with scale modulation from catastrophic (-{Z}pp at 0.5B) to negligible at 7B. Detection signal Min-K++ actively reverses direction at small scales (Cohen's d = {W}), making single-signal contamination detection unreliable. Fisher Information analysis establishes that reliable γ recovery requires N≥50 models; recovery succeeded down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested), with the lower bound remaining open — conditions rarely met in current LLM evaluation.\"\n47\t\n48\t## Venue Fit Risks\n49\t- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n50\t- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n51\t- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n52\t- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n53\t\n54\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n55\t\n56\t| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n57\t|---|---------|---------|------------|\n58\t| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 20-condition simulation sweep (200 runs) + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n59\t| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n60\t| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n61\t| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n62\t| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n63\t| 6 | Too few models (N≈15)? | D025 multi-seed strategy: 3 scales × 4+ dosages × 2-3 seeds = 24-36 models pooled. Target N≥30 minimum (N≥50 ideal). Simulation sweep (exp-004) establishes identifiability at actual N. Growing Pains pre-estimation as backup at very low N | exp-004, exp-001 |\n64\t| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n65\t| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n66\t| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n67\t| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n68\t| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n69\t| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n70\t| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n71\t| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n72\t| 15 | Novelty ceiling — standard IRT synthesis? (D025, all 3 Dream tiers) | Positioned as cross-disciplinary synthesis, NOT new algorithm. Emergent value: (a) per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity as benchmark property, (b) Min-K++ reversal as cautionary finding, (c) Fisher Info identifiability boundaries. If 4PL vs 2PL gap small → acknowledge honestly but argue c/d params needed for realistic MC modeling. Report oracle-γ ablation to decompose MAE. | exp-006 |\n73\t| 16 | Per-benchmark γ_k via separate fits, not hierarchical? (D025, [M][W]) | Per-benchmark separate fits = primary (natural, avoids cross-benchmark correlation). Hierarchical γ_k ~ Normal(μ_γ, σ_γ) as ablation to formally test heterogeneity and share info across benchmarks. Both reported. | exp-006 |\n74\t| 17 | Coverage gap — 85% vs 95% nominal? (D025, all 3 tiers) | SBC diagnostic to identify source. Possible causes: local independence violation, insufficient N, model misspecification. Report SBC alongside posterior predictive checks. If gap is from local independence → discuss as known IRT limitation with references. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n75\t| 18 | Why not just S1 alone? (D025, [M][W]) | S1-only is a baseline (#2 single-signal 2PL+γ). If RRF ≤ S1-only → present as \"S1+IRT calibration with extensible multi-signal architecture for future signals.\" Meta-circularity argument for training-free aggregation still holds. | exp-003 |\n76\t| 19 | Coverage collapse 20-33% even with oracle? (D036) | Far worse than #17 anticipated. PPP extremes (ARC=1.0, GSM8K=0.0) = systematic misspecification. Path: (a) increase N via multi-seed pooling, (b) check exp-004 sweep coverage at matching N, (c) if additive γ insufficient → item-level heterogeneity or hierarchical structure. Fallback: lead with MAE+ranking, present coverage honestly. | exp-006, exp-004 |\n77\t| 20 | GSM8K overcorrection — calibration 11× worse than raw (D039, all 3 dream tiers) | Honest negative result. Implement calibration gating: γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1,0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. Report raw scores when gating fails. Reframe: ContamCal identifies when NOT to calibrate. | exp-006 |\n78\t| 21 | Linear regression nearly as good as IRT (D039, all 3 tiers) | B4 MAE=0.040 vs ContamCal MAE=0.035 on ARC. IRT's value is NOT better MAE — it's interpretable per-item parameters, benchmark-dependent γ_k characterization, and theoretical grounding. Bootstrap CI on MAE gap to show significance (or acknowledge non-significance). | exp-006 |\n79\t| 22 | SFT capacity artifact ≠ contamination (D039, all 3 tiers) | 0.5B ARC forgetting (-17pp) may be capacity displacement, not contamination-specific. Need: (a) SFT-on-random-data control at 0.5B to isolate capacity vs contamination, (b) focus on 1.5B/3B where capacity effects are minimal (<2pp). Acknowledge 0.5B forgetting as combined SFT+contamination effect. | exp-001 |\n80\t| 23 | N=8/16 contradicts paper's own N≥50 recommendation (D039, all 3 tiers) | Internal contradiction. Path: (a) add N=8 condition to simulation sweep for calibrated expectations, (b) increase N via multi-seed (target N≥30), (c) if N<30 → explicitly position as preliminary with caveats. SBC diagnostic at actual N to decompose coverage gap. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n81\t\n82\t## Evaluation Protocol (6 Metrics — D025 added SBC)\n83\t1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n84\t2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n85\t3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n86\t4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n87\t5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n88\t6. **SBC diagnostic** (D025): Simulation-Based Calibration (Talts et al. 2018) at actual N_models and N_items to diagnose coverage gap source (model misspecification vs insufficient N vs local independence violation). CPU-only, parallelize with GPU experiments\n89\t\n90\t## Benchmarks (2 main + 2 contrasts)\n91\t1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items, subsampled 1000 for NUTS) — PRIMARY\n92\t2. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice (~1.2K items) — PRIMARY. ⚠️ D036: gamma consistently negative (forgetting, not inflation). 3 independent evidence chains: s42 raw (-17.5pp), s123 raw (-10.2pp), oracle IRT (γ=-2.83). Scale-dependent: 3B d100 ARC=84% (mild forgetting) vs 0.5B d100 ARC=29-37% (catastrophic). Paper frames as \"benchmark-dependent contamination effects\" — ContamCal calibrates in both directions.\n93\t3. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items) — NEGATIVE CONTROL (D024: open-ended tasks naturally resist contamination; signals non-discriminative; IRT correctly finds γ≈0. Paper frames as \"ContamCal identifies benchmarks not requiring calibration\")\n94\t4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation (N=164). Demoted D009: paraphrase QC 16%.\n95\t\n96\t## Baselines (7)\n97\t0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n98\t1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n99\t2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n100\t3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n101\t4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n102\t5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n103\t6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n104\t\n105\t## Local Independence Discussion\n106\tStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n107\t\n108\t## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n109\t1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n110\t2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n111\t3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n112\t4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n113\t5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n114\t\n115\t## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\n116\tMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\n117\tDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n118\t- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n119\t- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n120\t- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n121\t- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n122\t- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\n123\tD025 additions (all 3 Dream tiers flagged):\n124\t- MIA foundations: Shokri et al. 2017 (membership inference attacks), Carlini et al. 2022 (extracting training data) — contextualize Min-K++ reversal in MIA literature\n125\t- Alternative evaluation: HELM (Liang et al. 2023), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) — discuss in Related Work as contamination-bypassing alternatives\n126\t- Deeper DCR engagement: Oren et al. 2024 structural comparison\n127\t- Burnell et al. 2023: multidimensional IRT for LLMs (relevant to unidimensional θ limitation)\n128\t\n129\t## Discussion Notes\n130\t\n131\t### D045: 0.5B Position Bias and MC Benchmark Exclusion (2026-05-15)\n132\t- **Finding**: Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias in logprob-based MC evaluation. MMLU prediction distribution: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%. SFT contamination amplifies pre-existing position prior rather than encoding knowledge.\n133\t- **Decision**: Exclude 0.5B MMLU/ARC data from IRT pooling. Retain 0.5B GSM8K/HumanEval (free-form, unaffected). 1.5B/3B all benchmarks normal.\n134\t- **Paper treatment**: Report in Discussion as \"capacity threshold for reliable MC evaluation.\" The 0.5B dosage-accuracy decline (40→23% MMLU) is position bias amplification, not knowledge degradation. This reinforces Concern #22 (SFT capacity artifact) and motivates the 1.5B+ focus in main results.\n135\t- **Broader implication**: Logprob-based MC evaluation at small scales may be unreliable across model families — note as limitation and suggest token-level or generation-based alternatives for sub-1B models.\n136\t\n137\t## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\n138\tMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\n139\tThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\n140\tThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n141\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"baf1ebfd-c40a-48bb-8a94-23648365c3ce","uuid":"4ebdf884-3cbf-46d6-b2a0-c06e90ffda29","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:37:25.703Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n\n## Target Venue\n- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n\n## Core Insight (D039 pivot + D108/D115 domain heterogeneity — N=24 confirmed)\nBenchmark contamination effects are far more complex than \"scores go up.\" Through controlled SFT injection and contamination-augmented IRT analysis, we reveal: effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks (MMLU: Simpson's paradox — aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM-gradient pattern where factual-knowledge domains show strong positive γ while non-STEM domains exhibit model misfit), are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks (ARC: forgetting vs GSM8K: inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic at 0.5B, negligible at 7B), and detection signals exhibit fundamental failure modes (Min-K++ directional reversal). ContamCal uses IRT with per-benchmark γ_k to systematically characterize these behaviors and establish identifiability boundaries. Calibration is possible conditionally — when model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) and signal quality is sufficient (recovery validated down to AUC=0.55, lowest tested, at J≥50) — but the primary contribution is the characterization framework itself. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004).\n\n## Core Claims (4 条 — D039 reorder: characterization first, calibration demoted)\n\n1. **Characterization Claim (PRIMARY — D108/D115: domain heterogeneity confirmed at N=24)**: Contamination effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks, are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks, and scale-dependent. Within MMLU, stratification by cognitive domain (57 subjects → 8 domains) reveals Simpson's paradox: aggregate γ≈0 masks a clear STEM gradient. **N=24 confirmed results (D115)**:\n   - STEM_Math: γ=+0.969, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.438 ✓ (strong positive — contamination inflates factual recall)\n   - STEM_Science: γ=+0.838, CI excludes 0, PPP=0.807 ✓ (strong positive)\n   - STEM_CS_Eng: γ=+0.197, CI includes 0, PPP=0.892 ✓ (null — procedural/applied knowledge less affected)\n   - Non-STEM (Medical, Humanities, Social_Sciences, Business_Law): all PPP≈1.0 → 2PL+γ model structurally misspecified. Negative γ estimates (-0.17 to -0.50) reflect model assumption failure, not true contamination direction.\n   - \"Other\" domain: system crash (disk/cgroup), non-essential catch-all category.\n   **Interpretation**: Contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge density — domains rich in memorizable facts (Math, Science) show large positive γ (SFT exposure → recall boost → inflated scores). Procedural/applied domains (CS_Eng) show weaker effect. Non-STEM domains require richer models (multidimensional IRT or domain-specific covariates) — reported as acknowledged limitation and future work.\n   **Paper claim scope**: Domain heterogeneity is established via (a) Simpson's paradox (aggregate ≈ 0, domain-level ≠ 0), (b) STEM gradient with good model fit (3/8 domains with PPP∈[0.1,0.9]), (c) non-STEM misfit as structural boundary of 2PL+γ. Claims based on domains with acceptable PPP ONLY. Across benchmarks: ARC forgetting (γ < 0, CI excludes zero, 3 independent evidence chains) vs GSM8K inflation (γ > 0). Effects are scale-modulated: catastrophic at 0.5B (-17pp ARC), mild at 3B, negligible at 7B. This challenges the dominant \"contamination = uniform inflation\" narrative. Cross-family consistency with Llama-3.2-3B (pending eval completion) tests whether contamination susceptibility is a benchmark × domain property, not a model family artifact.\n   **PPP presentation strategy**: Non-STEM PPP=1.0 is not a failure of the analysis but a meaningful boundary finding: 2PL+γ (additive contamination effect on unidimensional ability) captures STEM domain mechanics but not non-STEM. Discuss connection to multidimensional IRT (Reckase 2009) and DIF literature (Holland & Wainer 1993). Consider limited-information fit indices (M2*, RMSEA2) as robustness check for STEM results.\n\n2. **Signal Failure Claim (CO-PRIMARY, headline finding per D039 dream 三档一致)**: Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at 0.5B d100) — actively misleading, not merely ineffective. S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Multi-signal RRF degrades to S1-only in practice — paper is transparent about this (D039: all 3 dream tiers require honesty here). Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design (meta-circularity argument). Must distinguish item-level detection AUC from model-level Cohen's d.\n   **C2 contingency**: \"Scale-dependent\" unverified beyond 0.5B. If 1.5B/3B S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness.\" Min-K++ reversal remains novel regardless.\n\n3. **Identifiability Claim**: Fisher Information analysis + simulation sweep (20 conditions, 200 runs). Recovery succeeds down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested) at N≥50; the lower bound remains open. LLM evaluation inherently operates below N≥50 (typically N=8-30), creating a structural domain-method mismatch — this is the real \"impossibility,\" not merely \"IRT needs more data.\" CI width ∝ N^{-0.48} (simulation). Coverage collapse at N<30 confirmed empirically (D036). D039 dream worst-tier caution: frame as domain mismatch insight, not as surprising impossibility.\n\n4. **Conditional Calibration Claim (DEMOTED from old Claim 1 per D039)**: When IRT model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) AND signal quality sufficient (validated down to AUC=0.55 at J≥50), calibration produces usable point estimates (ARC MAE=0.035). Coverage remains below nominal (20-69% at 95%) — present calibrated CIs as directional indicators, not precise credible intervals. Calibration gating criterion (D039 dream recommendation): γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1, 0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. GSM8K overcorrection (MAE 11× worse than raw) is an honest negative result demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity.\n\n## Contributions (Hierarchical — D039 rebalanced per Dream 2nd round)\n\n### Core (characterization-led; calibration conditional)\n1. **Domain-dependent contamination characterization** via IRT γ_k: Simpson's paradox within MMLU (aggregate γ≈0 masks STEM gradient: Math +0.97 > Science +0.84 >> CS_Eng ≈0), scale modulation, three-regime taxonomy (forgetting / selective memorization / near-immunity). γ heterogeneity maps to factual knowledge density. Non-STEM model misfit as boundary finding (2PL+γ scope limitation)\n2. **Signal failure characterization**: Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7) — paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding (D025 + D039 dream 三档一致). Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping. Honest reporting that RRF = S1-only in practice\n3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: structural vs practical thresholds with domain-method mismatch insight. Strongest theoretical contribution (D025 + D039 一致)\n\n### Supporting\n4. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (S1 provenance → contamination-augmented IRT with per-benchmark γ_k). Conditional calibration with gating criterion. ME model as ablation\n5. Dual-track validation + 7 baselines + 6 metrics (incl. SBC). Controlled SFT injection (internal) + ecological RL (external)\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n- **Our framing (D039 pivot)**: ContamCal is a diagnostic framework that uses IRT to systematically characterize how contamination affects benchmark scores. Rather than claiming to \"fix\" contaminated scores, we reveal the structure of contamination effects — they are bidirectional, scale-dependent, and benchmark-specific — and establish the conditions under which statistical calibration is and isn't feasible. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution, (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores, (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability.\n- **Headline result template (D039 — characterization-led)**: \"Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent: IRT γ captures forgetting on ARC (γ={X}, CI excludes zero) and inflation on GSM8K (γ={Y}), with scale modulation from catastrophic (-{Z}pp at 0.5B) to negligible at 7B. Detection signal Min-K++ actively reverses direction at small scales (Cohen's d = {W}), making single-signal contamination detection unreliable. Fisher Information analysis establishes that reliable γ recovery requires N≥50 models; recovery succeeded down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested), with the lower bound remaining open — conditions rarely met in current LLM evaluation.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n\n| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n|---|---------|---------|------------|\n| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 20-condition simulation sweep (200 runs) + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n| 6 | Too few models (N≈15)? | D025 multi-seed strategy: 3 scales × 4+ dosages × 2-3 seeds = 24-36 models pooled. Target N≥30 minimum (N≥50 ideal). Simulation sweep (exp-004) establishes identifiability at actual N. Growing Pains pre-estimation as backup at very low N | exp-004, exp-001 |\n| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n| 15 | Novelty ceiling — standard IRT synthesis? (D025, all 3 Dream tiers) | Positioned as cross-disciplinary synthesis, NOT new algorithm. Emergent value: (a) per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity as benchmark property, (b) Min-K++ reversal as cautionary finding, (c) Fisher Info identifiability boundaries. If 4PL vs 2PL gap small → acknowledge honestly but argue c/d params needed for realistic MC modeling. Report oracle-γ ablation to decompose MAE. | exp-006 |\n| 16 | Per-benchmark γ_k via separate fits, not hierarchical? (D025, [M][W]) | Per-benchmark separate fits = primary (natural, avoids cross-benchmark correlation). Hierarchical γ_k ~ Normal(μ_γ, σ_γ) as ablation to formally test heterogeneity and share info across benchmarks. Both reported. | exp-006 |\n| 17 | Coverage gap — 85% vs 95% nominal? (D025, all 3 tiers) | SBC diagnostic to identify source. Possible causes: local independence violation, insufficient N, model misspecification. Report SBC alongside posterior predictive checks. If gap is from local independence → discuss as known IRT limitation with references. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n| 18 | Why not just S1 alone? (D025, [M][W]) | S1-only is a baseline (#2 single-signal 2PL+γ). If RRF ≤ S1-only → present as \"S1+IRT calibration with extensible multi-signal architecture for future signals.\" Meta-circularity argument for training-free aggregation still holds. | exp-003 |\n| 19 | Coverage collapse 20-33% even with oracle? (D036) | Far worse than #17 anticipated. PPP extremes (ARC=1.0, GSM8K=0.0) = systematic misspecification. Path: (a) increase N via multi-seed pooling, (b) check exp-004 sweep coverage at matching N, (c) if additive γ insufficient → item-level heterogeneity or hierarchical structure. Fallback: lead with MAE+ranking, present coverage honestly. | exp-006, exp-004 |\n| 20 | GSM8K overcorrection — calibration 11× worse than raw (D039, all 3 dream tiers) | Honest negative result. Implement calibration gating: γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1,0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. Report raw scores when gating fails. Reframe: ContamCal identifies when NOT to calibrate. | exp-006 |\n| 21 | Linear regression nearly as good as IRT (D039, all 3 tiers) | B4 MAE=0.040 vs ContamCal MAE=0.035 on ARC. IRT's value is NOT better MAE — it's interpretable per-item parameters, benchmark-dependent γ_k characterization, and theoretical grounding. Bootstrap CI on MAE gap to show significance (or acknowledge non-significance). | exp-006 |\n| 22 | SFT capacity artifact ≠ contamination (D039, all 3 tiers) | 0.5B ARC forgetting (-17pp) may be capacity displacement, not contamination-specific. Need: (a) SFT-on-random-data control at 0.5B to isolate capacity vs contamination, (b) focus on 1.5B/3B where capacity effects are minimal (<2pp). Acknowledge 0.5B forgetting as combined SFT+contamination effect. | exp-001 |\n| 23 | N=8/16 contradicts paper's own N≥50 recommendation (D039, all 3 tiers) | Internal contradiction. Path: (a) add N=8 condition to simulation sweep for calibrated expectations, (b) increase N via multi-seed (target N≥30), (c) if N<30 → explicitly position as preliminary with caveats. SBC diagnostic at actual N to decompose coverage gap. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n\n## Evaluation Protocol (6 Metrics — D025 added SBC)\n1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n6. **SBC diagnostic** (D025): Simulation-Based Calibration (Talts et al. 2018) at actual N_models and N_items to diagnose coverage gap source (model misspecification vs insufficient N vs local independence violation). CPU-only, parallelize with GPU experiments\n\n## Benchmarks (2 main + 2 contrasts)\n1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items, subsampled 1000 for NUTS) — PRIMARY\n2. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice (~1.2K items) — PRIMARY. ⚠️ D036: gamma consistently negative (forgetting, not inflation). 3 independent evidence chains: s42 raw (-17.5pp), s123 raw (-10.2pp), oracle IRT (γ=-2.83). Scale-dependent: 3B d100 ARC=84% (mild forgetting) vs 0.5B d100 ARC=29-37% (catastrophic). Paper frames as \"benchmark-dependent contamination effects\" — ContamCal calibrates in both directions.\n3. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items) — NEGATIVE CONTROL (D024: open-ended tasks naturally resist contamination; signals non-discriminative; IRT correctly finds γ≈0. Paper frames as \"ContamCal identifies benchmarks not requiring calibration\")\n4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation (N=164). Demoted D009: paraphrase QC 16%.\n\n## Baselines (7)\n0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n\n## Local Independence Discussion\nStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n\n## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n\n## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\nMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\nDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\nD025 additions (all 3 Dream tiers flagged):\n- MIA foundations: Shokri et al. 2017 (membership inference attacks), Carlini et al. 2022 (extracting training data) — contextualize Min-K++ reversal in MIA literature\n- Alternative evaluation: HELM (Liang et al. 2023), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) — discuss in Related Work as contamination-bypassing alternatives\n- Deeper DCR engagement: Oren et al. 2024 structural comparison\n- Burnell et al. 2023: multidimensional IRT for LLMs (relevant to unidimensional θ limitation)\n\n## Discussion Notes\n\n### D045: 0.5B Position Bias and MC Benchmark Exclusion (2026-05-15)\n- **Finding**: Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias in logprob-based MC evaluation. MMLU prediction distribution: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%. SFT contamination amplifies pre-existing position prior rather than encoding knowledge.\n- **Decision**: Exclude 0.5B MMLU/ARC data from IRT pooling. Retain 0.5B GSM8K/HumanEval (free-form, unaffected). 1.5B/3B all benchmarks normal.\n- **Paper treatment**: Report in Discussion as \"capacity threshold for reliable MC evaluation.\" The 0.5B dosage-accuracy decline (40→23% MMLU) is position bias amplification, not knowledge degradation. This reinforces Concern #22 (SFT capacity artifact) and motivates the 1.5B+ focus in main results.\n- **Broader implication**: Logprob-based MC evaluation at small scales may be unreliable across model families — note as limitation and suggest token-level or generation-based alternatives for sub-1B models.\n\n## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\nMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\nThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\nThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n","numLines":141,"startLine":1,"totalLines":141}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QoaBB1Z4G2FPkJAWoe4aQb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me analyze the data carefully and write a complete LaTeX paper.\n\nThe paper is a \"worst-tier\" dream paper — meaning it frames results as negative/diagnostic findings. The narrative guidance says to reframe as a diagnostic framework paper.\n\nKey experiments:\n1. **sft_d100_qc_only** - QC filtering null result at 0.5B\n2. **scale_bridging_7b** - 7B shows near-zero contamination effects, calibration worse than raw\n3. **multi_family_validation** - Llama-3.2-3B shows different patterns than Qwen, cross-family γ not correlated\n\n\n4. **rl_observational_case_study** - Complete calibration failure on real-world models\n5. **observational_72b** - 72B frontier model, detection signals degrade, calibration useless\n6. **closed_weight_2signal_mode** - Without model weights, the pipeline breaks down entirely\n\nThe narrative arc shows ContamCal was designed to calibrate contaminated benchmarks, but instead revealed why this is fundamentally intractable. Three diagnostic insights emerge: Min-K++ directional reversal under contamination, Fisher Information identifiability requiring N≥50 samples when LLMs operate at N=8-30, and contamination effects that vary by benchmark but follow domain-specific patterns.\n\nNow I'm structuring the paper with standard sections—abstract, introduction, methods covering the ContamCal framework, experiments across all six cases, results and discussion, and conclusion. Writing the LaTeX document...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17852,"cache_read_input_tokens":23189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17852},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":